// The SPI controller.
//
// It has one shift engine and one register bank, and it can be driven two ways:
// the processor moving every word through SPI_TX / SPI_RX, or the DMA moving
// them in response to the controller's FIFO request lines. Both styles are
// modelled, and the state object below is what keeps a scenario from mixing
// them up.
//
// The skeleton only (coding guidelines rule 3): members, pools, and the
// solve-time constructor. The operations are in spi_c/functions/ and their
// scenario wrappers in spi_c/actions/.
import addr_reg_pkg::*;
import spi_regs_pkg::*;
import spi_types_pkg::*;

component spi_c {

    // The MMIO bank. A top-level register group: bound once, from the
    // environment's init exec, by way of initialize() below.
    spi_regs_c        regs;

    // The controller's current configuration.
    //
    // PER CONTROLLER, which is what declaring the pool here rather than in
    // pss_top buys: two spi_c instances configured differently do not fight
    // over one state object. A state pool holds exactly one object at a time,
    // so this is "the mode the device is in", and every transfer action reads
    // it.
    pool spi_state_s  state_p;
    bind state_p *;

    // The shift engine: one, so at most one transfer at a time.
    //
    // Size 1 IS the exclusion. Every transfer action locks it, so a programmed
    // transfer and a DMA transfer on the same controller can never overlap --
    // and neither can two DMA transfers in opposite directions, even though
    // they use different DMA channels. That last one is the case a
    // channel-only model gets wrong.
    pool [1] spi_engine_s  eng_p;
    bind eng_p *;

    // Bind the bank to the region the system put this controller in. Called
    // from the environment's init_down exec: set_handle() is legal only from an
    // init exec, and component attributes are writable only during elaboration.
    solve function void initialize(addr_handle_t base) {
        regs.set_handle(base);
    }
}
